export const DATA_61: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-080", num: 80, title: "Đề 080", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Understanding Culture Shock", paragraphs: ["Every year, millions of people move to a new country for work, education, or a fresh start. Many of them are surprised to discover that life abroad is far more challenging than they expected — not because of language barriers or practical difficulties, but because of a deeper feeling of disorientation and loss. This experience has a name: culture shock. Although it is not a medical condition, culture shock is a very real psychological response to living in an unfamiliar environment, and understanding it can help people manage the transition to a new culture more successfully.", "Culture shock typically follows a pattern that psychologists describe in four stages. The first stage is often called the \"honeymoon phase.\" During this period, everything in the new country feels exciting, fresh, and interesting. A person may feel thrilled by the new foods, architecture, and customs they encounter, and differences from home seem charming rather than troubling. This phase can last anywhere from a few days to several months, depending on the individual and the circumstances of their move.", "The second stage, sometimes called the \"frustration\" or \"crisis\" stage, is when culture shock truly sets in. The novelty of the new environment begins to wear off, and the traveller starts to notice — and be bothered by — things that are different from what they are used to. Simple tasks like shopping, using public transport, or making friends can feel unexpectedly difficult. The person may become irritable, homesick, and critical of the local culture, sometimes idealising their home country in contrast. This stage is the most emotionally difficult and is the point at which many people consider abandoning their experience abroad and returning home.", "Fortunately, most people do not remain in the frustration stage forever. The third stage is one of gradual adjustment , during which the individual begins to develop coping strategies and a deeper understanding of the new culture. Routines become more familiar, communication improves, and the person starts to find genuine enjoyment in aspects of the local life. Friendships with local people and other expatriates can be particularly helpful during this period, providing emotional support and practical guidance. By the end of this stage, the individual generally feels much more comfortable and functional in their new environment.", "The fourth and final stage is often described as \"adaptation\" or \"integration.\" At this point, the person has developed a genuine appreciation of the new culture and feels at home in both their original culture and the new one. They may still prefer certain aspects of their home country, but they can navigate the new environment with confidence and even come to value aspects of it that once seemed strange or difficult. Some people reach this stage within months; for others, it takes years. A small number never fully reach this stage and continue to feel like outsiders, though this is less common when people actively engage with the local community.", "It is important to understand that culture shock affects people differently. Some individuals experience all four stages clearly, while others skip certain stages or cycle through them more than once. Factors such as age, personality, language ability, and the degree of cultural difference between the home and host country all influence how intensely a person experiences culture shock. Research suggests that people who are more open-minded, flexible, and willing to learn about the local culture tend to adapt more quickly than those who prefer to stay within communities of people from their home country.", "There are several practical strategies that can help ease the experience of culture shock. Learning the local language — even at a basic level — is one of the most effective ways to reduce feelings of isolation and build connections with local people. Maintaining regular contact with friends and family back home, while also making an effort to form new relationships in the host country, helps people feel supported during the difficult adjustment period. Keeping a journal, exercising regularly, and maintaining a sense of humour about cultural misunderstandings are all small habits that experienced expatriates frequently recommend. Most importantly, accepting that discomfort and confusion are a normal part of the process — and not a sign of personal failure — can make the experience of culture shock significantly easier to bear."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["A comparison of life in different countries and the challenges of international travel", "A study of why some people are better suited to living abroad than others", "An explanation of what culture shock is, how it develops in stages, and how people can manage it", "Advice for students who are planning to study in a foreign country for the first time"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what characterises the first stage of culture shock?", options: ["The person feels homesick and frequently compares the new country unfavourably with their home", "The person begins to develop friendships and routines that help them feel more settled", "The person finds the new environment exciting and sees cultural differences as charming rather than troubling", "The person feels confused and frustrated by small cultural differences in their daily life"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is the frustration stage considered the most difficult part of culture shock?", options: ["Because language barriers make it impossible to carry out daily tasks without help from local people", "Because the excitement of the honeymoon phase suddenly disappears and is replaced by emotional difficulties", "Because the person is legally required to return home if they cannot adapt within a set time period", "Because friends and family back home are unable to understand or support the person's experience"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role do friendships play during the adjustment stage of culture shock?", options: ["They give the person opportunities to teach others about their home culture and traditions", "They are the primary reason most people decide to remain in a foreign country permanently", "They provide emotional support and practical guidance that help the person adjust more successfully", "They allow the person to avoid interacting with the local culture until they feel fully ready"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor that affects how intensely a person experiences culture shock?", options: ["The amount of money the person has available to spend on entertainment and socialising", "The degree of cultural difference between the home and host country", "The person's ability to speak the local language", "The person's age and personality"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a practical strategy for coping with culture shock?", options: ["Avoiding contact with people from the home country to encourage faster integration", "Spending as little time as possible outside the home during the first few months abroad", "Keeping a journal and maintaining a sense of humour about cultural misunderstandings", "Enrolling immediately in a full-time language course before attempting to socialise"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the foods, architecture, and customs found in the new country", "differences between the home country and the new environment", "a person who has recently moved to a new and unfamiliar country", "psychologists who study and describe the stages of culture shock"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"adjustment\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a sudden and complete change in behaviour that happens without any conscious effort", "the decision to permanently abandon one's original cultural identity", "the process of gradually becoming more comfortable and capable in a new situation", "a formal agreement between a person and their host country about cultural expectations"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"adapt\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to spend a long time carefully observing others before making any social contact", "to successfully adjust to and function comfortably within a new environment", "to reject the values of the home culture in favour of the new one entirely", "to complain openly about aspects of the new culture that seem strange or unfair"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the experience of culture shock?", options: ["Indifferent — the author presents facts about culture shock without offering any personal perspective", "Discouraging — the author suggests that most people never fully recover from the experience", "Critical — the author believes culture shock is caused by poor preparation before moving abroad", "Reassuring and practical — the author presents culture shock as a normal process that most people can successfully navigate"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Hidden Problems of Imported Products", paragraphs: ["In an era of global trade, the shelves of supermarkets and online marketplaces are filled with products sourced from every corner of the world. Consumers in high-income countries enjoy a variety of goods — from fresh fruit and clothing to electronics and furniture — that would have been unimaginable to previous generations. The apparent abundance and low prices of imported products are widely celebrated as one of globalisation's greatest achievements. Yet behind this convenience lies a series of hidden problems that affect workers, environments, and societies in both producing and consuming countries — problems that the low price tag on a product rarely reflects .", "One of the most significant concerns is labour exploitation. In many countries that produce goods for export — particularly in the garment, electronics, and food processing industries — workers endure long hours, unsafe conditions, and wages that fall well below what would be considered acceptable in the countries where those goods are ultimately sold. The collapse of the Rana Plaza garment factory in Bangladesh in 2013, which killed over 1,100 workers, drew international attention to the dangerous working conditions that can exist deep within global supply chains. Although that disaster prompted some improvements in factory safety standards in Bangladesh, critics argue that the structural economic pressures that lead companies to seek the cheapest possible production sites have not fundamentally changed, and that exploitation continues in less visible forms across many industries and regions.", "Environmental damage is another hidden cost of imported goods. The transportation of products across oceans and continents generates enormous quantities of greenhouse gases: the shipping industry alone accounts for approximately three percent of global carbon emissions, and this figure does not include the emissions produced during manufacturing, packaging, or final delivery to the consumer. In producing countries, the environmental consequences can be even more severe. Agricultural exports often involve the conversion of forests and wetlands into farmland, destroying biodiversity and releasing stored carbon. Mining operations that supply minerals for electronics manufacture have been linked to water contamination, soil degradation, and the displacement of local communities. These environmental costs are borne primarily by the communities and ecosystems closest to production, while the benefits of consumption accrue to people far removed from the damage.", "The economic effects of import dependency on domestic industries deserve attention as well. When a country relies heavily on imported goods — particularly manufactured products — its own industrial capacity can wither as domestic firms are unable to compete with lower-cost foreign producers. This process, sometimes called \"deindustrialisation,\" has affected manufacturing communities in the United States, the United Kingdom, and other high-income countries over the past four decades, resulting in significant job losses, the decline of once-thriving industrial towns, and long-term social problems including unemployment, reduced tax revenues, and weakened community cohesion. While economists often argue that the benefits of free trade outweigh these costs at the aggregate level, the costs are not evenly distributed: they fall disproportionately on working-class communities in regions that were historically dependent on manufacturing employment.", "Food safety is a further dimension of the imported products problem that receives less public attention than it perhaps deserves. Products imported from countries with less stringent regulatory frameworks may not meet the food safety, chemical residue, or labelling standards that domestic producers are legally required to observe. Imported fresh produce, for example, may have been treated with pesticides that are banned in the importing country, or may have been stored or transported in conditions that accelerate bacterial growth. While border inspection and import regulations are designed to catch such problems, the sheer volume of imported goods makes comprehensive testing impractical, and contaminated products do occasionally enter the food supply. High-profile food safety incidents — such as the melamine contamination of Chinese dairy products in 2008, which affected consumers in multiple countries — have highlighted the vulnerability of global food supply chains to quality failures that travel rapidly across borders.", "Consumer choices and corporate accountability represent potential levers for addressing some of these problems. Certification schemes such as Fairtrade, Rainforest Alliance, and various labour standard auditing programmes attempt to give consumers a way to identify products that have been produced to higher social and environmental standards. However, the effectiveness of these schemes is contested: critics argue that certification processes are often costly, difficult to verify independently, and may create a misleading impression that certified supply chains are problem-free. Some researchers suggest that the most meaningful changes require not just consumer choice but structural policy interventions — including stronger import regulations, mandatory corporate due diligence requirements, and international trade agreements that include enforceable labour and environmental standards.", "Ultimately, the low price of an imported product rarely tells the full story of what it cost to bring that product to market. The hidden costs — paid by workers, communities, and ecosystems far from the point of sale — are real, significant, and increasingly well documented. Addressing them requires both greater transparency in supply chains and a willingness, on the part of governments, corporations, and consumers alike, to accept that genuinely fair trade may not always be the cheapest trade."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To explain how global trade agreements have contributed to economic growth in developing countries", "To compare the quality of imported goods with domestically produced alternatives", "To explore the hidden social, environmental, and economic costs associated with imported products", "To argue that consumers in high-income countries should stop buying imported goods entirely"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the Rana Plaza disaster in 2013 reveal about global supply chains?", options: ["That dangerous working conditions can exist deep within the supply chains that produce goods for wealthy consumers", "That international trade regulations were entirely ineffective at preventing workplace accidents in developing countries", "That garment factories in Bangladesh were producing goods that failed to meet quality standards in importing countries", "That the garment industry was the only sector in which workers faced serious risks to their health and safety"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an environmental consequence of producing imported goods?", options: ["Water contamination and soil degradation linked to mining operations for electronics minerals", "The destruction of forests and wetlands converted into agricultural land for export crops", "Greenhouse gas emissions generated by shipping products across oceans", "The extinction of marine species caused by increased shipping traffic in major ocean trade routes"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the economic impact of deindustrialisation on affected communities?", options: ["It caused short-term disruption but was quickly reversed as new manufacturing sectors emerged in affected regions", "It primarily affected upper-income communities whose businesses depended on industrial exports", "It led to job losses, declining industrial towns, and social problems including unemployment and reduced community cohesion", "It encouraged affected workers to retrain in new industries, ultimately strengthening local economies"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the melamine contamination incident of 2008 illustrate?", options: ["That Chinese manufacturers were deliberately targeting foreign consumers with unsafe products", "That quality failures in global food supply chains can spread rapidly and affect consumers in multiple countries", "That comprehensive border testing of all imported food products is both necessary and achievable", "That dairy products are more susceptible to contamination than other types of imported food"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one criticism raised against certification schemes such as Fairtrade?", options: ["Their processes are costly, hard to verify independently, and may give a misleadingly positive impression", "They focus exclusively on environmental standards while ignoring the labour rights of workers", "They are too expensive for small producers in developing countries to access and benefit from", "They have been found to actively reduce wages for workers in certified supply chains"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["manufactured products imported from lower-cost foreign producers", "working-class communities in regions historically dependent on manufacturing", "the costs of deindustrialisation, which fall disproportionately on working-class communities", "economists who argue that the overall benefits of free trade outweigh its costs"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["the social and environmental problems caused by irresponsible corporate behaviour in global supply chains", "the consumers who choose to purchase certified products as a way of expressing their values", "the structural policy interventions recommended by researchers as more effective than consumer choice alone", "certification schemes such as Fairtrade and Rainforest Alliance designed to identify ethically produced goods"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"conversion\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the scientific measurement of carbon stored in forest and wetland ecosystems", "the gradual improvement of degraded land through careful environmental management", "the legal process of registering agricultural land for commercial use", "the act of changing something from one form or purpose into another"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the author's view of low-priced imported goods?", options: ["The author believes that low prices reflect genuine manufacturing efficiency that benefits everyone equally", "The author implies that low prices on imported goods are often possible because the true human and environmental costs are not included in the price", "The author suggests that consumers are entirely responsible for the hidden costs of imported products through their purchasing decisions", "The author concludes that import regulations are already sufficiently strong to prevent the worst abuses in global supply chains"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Toxic Behavior on Social Media", paragraphs: ["Social media platforms were originally conceived as spaces for connection — digital gathering places where people could share ideas, maintain friendships, and participate in public life. Yet the reality of these platforms, for a significant proportion of their users, has come to include something far less benign: a persistent culture of hostility, harassment, and contempt that researchers, journalists, and platform companies themselves now routinely describe as \"toxicity.\" Understanding why toxic behaviour flourishes online, and what can realistically be done about it, has become one of the defining challenges of digital public life.", "Psychologists point to several interconnected factors that make online environments particularly fertile ground for hostile behaviour. Chief among them is what researchers call the \"online disinhibition effect\" — the tendency for individuals to behave in ways online that they would not consider acceptable in face-to-face interactions. The relative anonymity of many online environments, the absence of real-time social feedback such as facial expressions and tone of voice, and the physical distance between participants all reduce the psychological inhibitions that normally prevent people from expressing hostility overtly. Studies have found that even the simple act of typing rather than speaking reduces the emotional weight a person assigns to their words, making it easier to say things that would feel uncomfortable or cruel if delivered in person.", "Platform design plays an equally important role. Social media companies have historically built their products around engagement metrics — the number of likes, shares, comments, and time spent on the platform — because these measures correlate with advertising revenue. [1] Research conducted by Facebook's own data scientists, later made public through the whistleblower Frances Haugen in 2021, found that content which provoked anger and outrage consistently generated higher engagement than neutral or positive content. [2] This creates a structural incentive for the amplification of toxic content: algorithms designed to maximise engagement systematically surface the most inflammatory posts to the widest possible audiences, regardless of the harm they cause to individuals or public discourse. [3] The result is an information environment in which the most extreme and hostile voices are disproportionately amplified, while measured and constructive contributions are comparatively invisible. [4]", "The consequences of online toxicity for individuals are well documented. Targets of sustained harassment campaigns — which can involve coordinated attacks by large numbers of people, the public disclosure of private information such as home addresses (a practice known as \"doxxing\"), and threats of physical violence — frequently report severe psychological distress, including symptoms consistent with post-traumatic stress disorder. Women, racial and ethnic minorities, and LGBTQ+ individuals are disproportionately targeted in harassment campaigns, and research consistently shows that the experience of online harassment causes many members of these groups to self-censor, withdraw from public platforms, or abandon online participation entirely. The silencing effect of toxicity thus has direct consequences for the diversity and quality of public discourse: when certain voices are systematically driven out of online spaces, the range of perspectives available in those spaces is correspondingly narrowed.", "The societal implications extend beyond the harm to individuals. Toxic discourse online has been linked to the erosion of shared factual ground — the common understanding of basic facts that civil debate requires. When platforms amplify outrage-generating content and reduce the visibility of careful, evidence-based contributions, they create an environment in which misinformation and conspiracy theories can spread rapidly while corrections and nuanced arguments struggle to gain traction. Some researchers argue that the sustained exposure to high-conflict, partisan content that algorithmic feeds deliver may actively polarise users' political views, though the causal relationship between platform design and political polarisation remains a subject of ongoing debate. What is less contested is that toxic online environments can normalise aggressive, dehumanising language, with potential consequences for how people interact not only online but in physical social and political spaces.", "Responses to the problem of online toxicity have varied considerably. Platform companies have introduced a range of content moderation tools — automated filters, human review teams, community reporting mechanisms, and penalties ranging from temporary suspensions to permanent account bans — but the scale of content produced on major platforms makes comprehensive moderation practically impossible, and the application of community standards has been criticised for inconsistency, cultural bias, and the disproportionate removal of content by users from marginalised communities. Legislative responses have included the European Union's Digital Services Act, which came into force in 2024 and imposes new obligations on large platforms to assess and mitigate the systemic risks their services pose, including risks to public discourse and mental health.", "Researchers and advocates increasingly argue that the most durable solutions require redesigning the fundamental incentive structures of social media platforms — moving away from engagement maximisation toward metrics that reward constructive interaction, diverse exposure, and user well-being. Whether the commercial interests of platform companies can be aligned with such a transformation, without regulatory compulsion, remains deeply uncertain. What seems clear is that toxic behaviour online is not an inevitable feature of human communication in digital spaces; it is, to a significant degree, a product of specific design choices that can, in principle, be revised."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central focus of the passage?", options: ["An argument that all social media platforms should be shut down due to the harm they cause to public discourse", "A technical explanation of how social media algorithms detect and remove harmful content automatically", "An analysis of why toxic behaviour occurs on social media, its consequences, and what might be done about it", "A comparison of different countries' legal approaches to regulating harmful content on social media platforms"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the \"online disinhibition effect\" refer to?", options: ["The process by which social media platforms remove accounts that repeatedly violate community standards", "The pattern in which users behave more hostilely online than they would in face-to-face interactions", "The tendency for people to spend more time on social media than they intend because of addictive design features", "The way in which social media algorithms encourage users to engage with content that matches their existing beliefs"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor in how platform design contributes to toxic content?", options: ["Research showing that anger-provoking content generates higher user engagement than neutral content", "Algorithms that surface the most inflammatory posts to the widest possible audiences", "Engagement metrics that reward content generating high numbers of reactions and shares", "The deliberate hiring of employees to write provocative content that increases platform traffic"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one consequence of sustained online harassment for targeted individuals?", options: ["Many targeted individuals pursue legal action, which results in the permanent removal of their harassers from online platforms", "Most targeted individuals recover quickly from the psychological effects of harassment within a few weeks", "Targets of harassment typically increase their online activity in order to defend their reputations publicly", "Harassment campaigns cause some individuals to withdraw from online spaces, reducing the diversity of voices in public discourse"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraphs 4 and 5 together about the broader societal effects of online toxicity?", options: ["Online toxicity primarily harms only the individuals who are directly targeted and has little effect on broader society", "The effects of online toxicity are largely temporary and reverse themselves once platforms introduce effective moderation tools", "Toxic content online has been proven conclusively to cause political violence in societies with weak democratic institutions", "The silencing of certain voices and the amplification of extreme content collectively degrade the quality of public discourse and may deepen social divisions"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the European Union's Digital Services Act require large platforms to do?", options: ["Employ a minimum number of human content moderators proportional to the size of their user base in each country", "Remove all user-generated content that has received more than a specified number of negative reports within 24 hours", "Publish the full details of their recommendation algorithms so that independent researchers can assess their impact", "Assess and mitigate the systemic risks their services pose, including risks to public discourse and mental health"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["individuals who behave hostilely online despite being cooperative in person", "psychologists who study the causes of hostile behaviour in online environments", "face-to-face interactions in which hostility is constrained by social feedback", "several interconnected factors that make online environments prone to hostile behaviour"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["social media companies that profit from advertising revenue", "users who produce the most extreme and hostile posts on social media platforms", "Facebook data scientists whose research was made public in 2021", "algorithms designed to maximise engagement by surfacing inflammatory content"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward online toxicity and the prospects for addressing it?", options: ["Angry — the author blames social media companies entirely and argues they should face criminal penalties", "Cautiously constructive — the author acknowledges the difficulty of the problem while arguing it is a product of design choices that can be changed", "Optimistic — the author believes the Digital Services Act and other regulations will quickly resolve the issue", "Resigned — the author implies that human nature makes toxic behaviour on social media impossible to reduce"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 3? 'This financial logic means that maximising user engagement and minimising harmful content are, in practice, often conflicting goals.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Uncertain Future of Polar Bears", paragraphs: ["Of the many species that stand to lose most from a warming planet, the polar bear has become the most emblematic . [1] Images of lone bears drifting on shrinking ice floes have circulated so widely that the animal now functions as a kind of visual shorthand for the existential stakes of climate change, its individual plight standing in for the broader fate of the cryosphere and the planetary systems that depend on it. [2] Yet the popular image of the polar bear as a passive victim waiting silently for extinction arguably distorts the ecological reality: polar bears are behaviourally flexible, physiologically formidable predators whose relationship with a rapidly changing environment is more dynamic and more uncertain than either catastrophist predictions or dismissive reassurances have tended to suggest. [3] Disentangling what the science actually shows from what advocates and sceptics have each wished it to show is a task that requires careful attention to the heterogeneity of the evidence. [4]", "The ecological foundation of polar bear biology is sea ice. Polar bears are marine mammals — classified under the family Ursidae but dependent on the ocean's surface for their primary hunting platform — and their survival is intimately bound to the seasonal cycle of Arctic sea ice formation and retreat. The bears prey predominantly on ringed and bearded seals, ambushing them at breathing holes or haul-out sites on the ice surface. The timing and extent of sea ice directly determines how long bears can hunt each year and therefore how much fat they can accumulate to sustain themselves and their offspring through ice-free summer periods when food is largely unavailable. Sea ice in the Arctic has declined dramatically since satellite monitoring began in the late 1970s: the September minimum extent — the point of least coverage at the end of summer — has decreased by approximately 13 percent per decade, and multi-year ice, which provides a more stable platform than newly formed seasonal ice, has declined even more sharply.", "The demographic consequences for polar bear populations are, however, uneven and in some cases genuinely uncertain. Of the nineteen recognised subpopulations of polar bears distributed across the Arctic, the International Union for Conservation of Nature assessed in its most recent comprehensive review that four were declining, two were increasing, and the status of the remaining thirteen was either stable or insufficiently monitored to determine. The subpopulations experiencing the most severe declines — particularly those in the southern Beaufort Sea and western Hudson Bay, where the loss of sea ice has been most pronounced — show clear statistical signatures of reduced survival and reproductive rates consistent with nutritional stress. But other subpopulations, particularly those in the high Arctic archipelagos of Canada and Norway, have shown greater resilience, and some individual bears have been documented consuming terrestrial food sources — berries, bird eggs, and vegetation — that were not previously considered significant components of the polar bear diet.", "The question of whether terrestrial food sources can meaningfully compensate for the loss of marine prey is one of the most contested issues in polar bear ecology. Researchers who have studied the energetic content of terrestrial foods available in Arctic environments argue that these resources are insufficient to offset the caloric demands of an animal as large as a polar bear: a single bearded seal provides an adult bear with roughly the same energy as several thousand goose eggs, and the effort required to forage for terrestrial food burns energy that the animal cannot easily replace. Other researchers contend that documented cases of successful terrestrial feeding suggest a capacity for dietary flexibility that earlier models may have underestimated, and that the extent to which bears can adapt their behaviour to changing food availability is genuinely unknown. What both sides of this debate agree on is that the outcome will likely vary significantly between subpopulations depending on the local rate of sea ice loss and the availability of alternative food resources in each region.", "Climate projections add a further layer of uncertainty to the picture. The trajectory of Arctic sea ice over the coming decades depends critically on the trajectory of global greenhouse gas emissions — a variable determined not by ecological dynamics but by the economic, political, and technological choices of human societies. Under high-emission scenarios, the Arctic is projected to experience ice-free summers before mid-century, a development that most polar bear researchers regard as incompatible with the long-term survival of southern subpopulations. Under lower-emission scenarios, consistent with significant global decarbonisation, the timing of ice-free summers is pushed back sufficiently that some subpopulations may remain viable for considerably longer. The divergence in projected outcomes across emission scenarios is so large that statements about the polar bear's future that do not specify which scenario they are describing are, in a meaningful sense, incomplete.", "Conservation efforts for polar bears operate under these compounding uncertainties. Traditional management approaches — harvest quotas, protection of denning areas, regulation of disturbance from tourism and industrial development — address proximate stressors but cannot, by themselves, offset the overriding threat of habitat loss driven by global temperature rise. Some researchers have proposed more interventionist strategies, including the supplemental feeding of food-stressed bears and the preservation of genetic material for potential future use, but these approaches remain controversial and are regarded by many conservation biologists as measures that address symptoms rather than causes. The consensus position within the polar bear research community is that meaningful conservation of the species in the wild requires, above all else, substantial and sustained reductions in global greenhouse gas emissions — a goal whose realisation depends on decisions made far beyond the boundaries of any protected area or wildlife management programme.", "The polar bear's symbolic prominence in climate communication has thus been something of a double-edged instrument. By personalising a planetary crisis that can otherwise feel abstract and remote, the bear has helped mobilise concern and political will. But the simplification required to make an animal into a symbol has also, at times, distorted the scientific picture — producing both exaggerated claims of imminent species-wide extinction and dismissive counter-claims that use the complexity of the evidence to suggest the threat is less serious than it is. The bear's actual fate, like the fate of the climate system itself, depends on choices that have not yet been made."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Conservation management of polar bears has failed because it has focused on local threats while ignoring the global climate crisis", "The future of polar bears is genuinely uncertain, shaped by complex ecological dynamics, uneven population trends, and the trajectory of global emissions", "Polar bears are on the verge of extinction and only an immediate global ban on fossil fuels can prevent their disappearance", "The polar bear's symbolic role in climate communication has been so effective that it has made other endangered species invisible"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["satellite monitoring systems that have tracked sea ice extent since the late 1970s", "marine mammals as a broader category that includes several Arctic species", "ringed and bearded seals that polar bears ambush at breathing holes on the ice surface", "polar bears, whose survival depends on the seasonal cycle of Arctic sea ice"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraphs 3 and 4 together about why polar bear population outcomes differ between subpopulations?", options: ["Scientific monitoring of northern subpopulations is more advanced, making their populations appear more stable than they actually are", "The degree of sea ice loss and the local availability of alternative food sources vary between regions, producing different survival pressures", "Polar bears in northern subpopulations have been protected by stricter national hunting laws than those in southern areas", "Subpopulations in warmer regions have evolved faster metabolisms that allow them to survive on smaller amounts of food"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the main argument made by researchers who are sceptical that terrestrial foods can replace marine prey for polar bears?", options: ["The caloric content of available terrestrial foods is far too low to offset the energy demands of an animal as large as a polar bear", "Polar bears lack the digestive physiology needed to extract sufficient nutrition from plant-based and avian food sources", "Terrestrial foods contain toxins that are harmless to humans but accumulate to dangerous levels in polar bears", "The effort required to find terrestrial food in the Arctic tundra makes such foraging strategies impossible during winter months"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the divergence in projected outcomes across different emission scenarios imply?", options: ["That predictions about the polar bear's future are meaningless unless they specify which emissions pathway they are based on", "That scientific models of polar bear survival are too unreliable to be used as the basis for conservation policy", "That only the most pessimistic high-emission scenarios are scientifically credible and should be used in public communication", "That polar bears are likely to survive in the wild regardless of how quickly global greenhouse gas emissions are reduced"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do many conservation biologists regard supplemental feeding and genetic preservation as insufficient responses to polar bear decline?", options: ["Because they address the symptoms of the problem rather than its underlying cause, which is habitat loss driven by global warming", "Because supplemental feeding has been shown to make polar bears dependent on humans and unable to hunt independently", "Because these approaches are too expensive to implement at the scale required to protect all nineteen polar bear subpopulations", "Because international law prohibits direct human intervention in the management of wild polar bear populations"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a conservation strategy for polar bears?", options: ["The preservation of genetic material for potential future conservation use", "Harvest quotas that limit the number of bears that can legally be hunted", "Regulation of disturbance from tourism and industrial development near polar bear habitat", "The establishment of international treaties requiring countries to share data on polar bear population trends"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"emblematic\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["isolated, separated from other members of its species by environmental change", "representative or symbolic of something larger than itself", "controversial, generating strong disagreement among scientists and policymakers", "endangered, meaning at serious risk of extinction within the foreseeable future"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following best characterises the author's approach to the evidence about polar bears and climate change?", options: ["The author critically examines the evidence from multiple angles, resisting both catastrophist and dismissive simplifications in favour of a nuanced assessment", "The author accepts the most alarming projections without qualification, arguing that urgent action is needed before it is too late", "The author defers entirely to the consensus position of the polar bear research community without offering independent analysis", "The author dismisses conservation concerns as exaggerated and argues that polar bears are more resilient than researchers claim"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 1? 'This symbolic function, however powerful, sits in an uneasy relationship with the complexity of what polar bear science actually reveals.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-132", num: 132, title: "Đề 132", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Living with FOMO", paragraphs: ["Have you ever checked your phone in the middle of dinner because you were worried you might miss something important? This feeling has a name: FOMO, or the \"Fear of Missing Out.\" It describes the anxious feeling people get when they think other people are having fun, trying something new, or enjoying an experience that they themselves are not part of. FOMO is not a new emotion, but social media has made it much stronger and much more common than it used to be.", "Social media plays a huge role in spreading FOMO. When people scroll through photos and videos of friends at parties, on vacation, or trying a popular new restaurant, it is easy to feel left out, even if their own life is perfectly fine. Apps are also designed to keep users checking constantly, with notifications and updates that make people feel they must look right away or risk missing something. Over time, this constant checking can become a difficult habit to break.", "FOMO can affect both the mind and the body. People who often feel FOMO may become more anxious, have trouble sleeping, or feel less satisfied with their own lives, even when nothing is actually wrong. Some people spend money they had not planned to spend, just to join an event or buy something before it runs out. Others feel pressure to say yes to every invitation, which can leave them feeling tired and stressed instead of happy.", "Fortunately, there are simple ways to manage FOMO. Turning off unnecessary notifications is one of the easiest first steps, since it removes the constant reminders that trigger the anxious feeling. Setting specific times to check social media, instead of checking it all day, can also help people feel more in control. These small changes do not require deleting social media completely, but they do help reduce how much power it has over a person's daily mood.", "Another helpful strategy is practicing gratitude , or taking time to notice and appreciate what is already good in one's own life. Experts suggest that people who regularly think about what they are thankful for tend to compare themselves to others less often. Spending real, face-to-face time with friends and family, rather than only interacting online, can also reduce feelings of loneliness that often make FOMO worse.", "In the end, FOMO is something almost everyone experiences from time to time, and feeling it occasionally is completely normal. The real problem begins only when FOMO starts to control daily decisions or damage a person's mental health. By becoming aware of the feeling and making small, consistent changes, most people can enjoy social media and other activities without letting the fear of missing out take over their lives."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage focuses only on the financial problems caused by impulsive spending.", "The passage mainly explains how social media companies design their notification systems.", "The passage argues that people should delete all social media apps to avoid FOMO completely.", "The passage explains what FOMO is, how social media makes it stronger, the effects it can have, and practical ways to manage it."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does FOMO describe?", options: ["A medical condition that only affects heavy social media users.", "The anxious feeling people get when they think others are enjoying something they are missing out on.", "The excitement people feel when attending a new event.", "A type of phone notification that reminds users to check social media."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do social media apps contribute to FOMO?", options: ["They require users to pay a fee to view friends' updates.", "They only show users content about restaurants and vacations.", "They automatically delete old photos and videos after a few days.", "They are designed to keep users checking constantly through notifications and updates that suggest something might be missed."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one effect FOMO can have on people?", options: ["It has no effect on sleep or spending habits.", "It always causes people to stop using social media completely.", "It can make people more anxious, cause trouble sleeping, or lead to unplanned spending.", "It guarantees that people will make new friends more easily."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what benefit does practicing gratitude offer?", options: ["It guarantees that a person will never feel FOMO again.", "It completely removes the need for face-to-face time with friends.", "It helps people earn more money over time.", "People who regularly think about what they are thankful for tend to compare themselves to others less often."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way to manage FOMO?", options: ["Paying for a premium social media subscription to remove ads.", "Setting specific times to check social media.", "Turning off unnecessary notifications.", "Spending real, face-to-face time with friends and family."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"trigger\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Hide", "Delay", "Cause", "Reduce"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"gratitude\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Confidence", "Patience", "Appreciation", "Curiosity"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"These small changes\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["Deleting social media accounts completely", "Turning off notifications and setting specific times to check social media", "Practicing gratitude every morning", "Spending money on new experiences"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about when FOMO becomes a serious concern?", options: ["FOMO becomes a concern the very first time someone feels left out of an event.", "FOMO only becomes serious once a person stops using social media entirely.", "FOMO is never a serious problem, according to the passage.", "FOMO becomes a genuine problem mainly when it starts influencing daily decisions or harming a person's mental health, rather than simply being felt occasionally."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Organic Farming and the Question of Sustainability", paragraphs: ["Organic agriculture, defined broadly as a system of farming that excludes synthetic pesticides, chemical fertilizers, and genetically modified organisms, has expanded considerably over the past two decades, moving from a niche market into a substantial segment of the global food industry. Proponents frequently present organic methods as inherently more sustainable than conventional farming, though the empirical picture, once examined closely, proves considerably more complicated than this framing suggests.", "One frequently cited advantage of organic farming concerns soil health. Practices such as crop rotation, composting, and the avoidance of synthetic chemical inputs are associated with higher levels of organic matter in the soil, which in turn tends to improve water retention and reduce erosion over time. Several long-term comparative studies have found that organically managed fields recover more quickly from drought conditions than conventionally managed fields nearby, a difference researchers attribute primarily to improved soil structure rather than to any single farming technique in isolation.", "Biodiversity outcomes tell a similarly favorable, though not unqualified, story. Organic farms generally support a greater abundance and variety of pollinators, birds, and beneficial insects than conventional farms, largely because the absence of broad-spectrum pesticides allows populations that would otherwise be suppressed to persist. This benefit, however, appears to depend considerably on the surrounding landscape; organic farms situated within otherwise heavily industrialized agricultural regions show smaller biodiversity gains than those located near existing natural habitat, suggesting that the advantage is not uniform across all contexts.", "Yield, by contrast, remains one of organic farming's most persistent challenges. Meta-analyses comparing organic and conventional systems have generally found that organic yields are lower, with the gap varying considerably by crop type—narrower for legumes and certain fruits, but often substantially wider for cereal grains such as wheat and rice. This yield gap matters considerably for any broader sustainability assessment, since lower yields imply that more land would be required to produce an equivalent quantity of food, potentially offsetting some of the environmental benefits achieved per unit of cultivated area.", "Certification and cost present a further complication rarely acknowledged in popular discussions of organic food. Achieving official organic certification typically requires several years of transition, during which farmers must comply with organic standards without yet being permitted to sell their products at organic prices, creating a financial burden that disproportionately affects smaller farming operations lacking the capital reserves to absorb several unprofitable transition years. As a result, some agricultural economists have argued that current certification structures may inadvertently favor larger, better-capitalized farms over the small independent producers the organic movement originally sought to support.", "Consumer perception, meanwhile, does not always align neatly with the underlying evidence. Surveys consistently find that a majority of consumers who purchase organic food do so primarily because they believe it is more nutritious than conventional food, yet nutritional comparisons have generally found only modest differences between the two, concentrated mainly in certain antioxidant compounds rather than in vitamins or minerals overall. This mismatch between consumer motivation and the actual weight of the evidence suggests that organic food's market success may rest as much on perception and values as on demonstrated nutritional advantage.", "Taken together, the evidence suggests that organic farming offers real, measurable environmental benefits in specific domains—soil health and biodiversity chief among them—while simultaneously facing genuine structural limitations, particularly around yield and equitable access to certification, that complicate any simple claim that it is unambiguously more sustainable than conventional agriculture in every respect."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage argues that organic farming is scientifically proven to be more sustainable than conventional farming in every measurable way.", "The passage focuses mainly on describing the certification process required for organic farms.", "The passage examines both the genuine environmental benefits and the structural limitations of organic farming, concluding that its overall sustainability is more complicated than commonly assumed.", "The passage claims that organic food has no meaningful benefits over conventional food."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do organic fields tend to recover more quickly from drought than conventional fields?", options: ["Practices like crop rotation and composting improve soil organic matter, which enhances water retention and reduces erosion over time.", "Several long-term comparative studies have found that organically managed fields recover more quickly from drought conditions.", "Organic fields receive significantly more rainfall than conventional fields located nearby.", "Organic farmers are legally required to install irrigation systems that conventional farmers cannot use."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what determines whether an organic farm shows strong biodiversity benefits?", options: ["Biodiversity benefits are identical across all organic farms regardless of their surrounding environment.", "The strength of the biodiversity benefit depends considerably on the surrounding landscape, with farms near natural habitat showing greater gains than those in heavily industrialized agricultural regions.", "Organic farms generally support a greater abundance and variety of pollinators, birds, and beneficial insects than conventional farms.", "Organic farms located near cities show the greatest biodiversity gains of any location type."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what financial challenge does organic certification create, and who is most affected?", options: ["Certification costs are equally burdensome for farms of all sizes, according to the passage.", "Farmers must meet organic standards for several years before being allowed to sell at organic prices, a burden that disproportionately affects smaller operations with limited capital reserves.", "Achieving official organic certification typically requires several years of transition.", "Large, well-capitalized farms are the only ones prevented from obtaining organic certification."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit associated with organic farming?", options: ["Consistently higher yields than conventional farming across all crop types.", "Higher levels of organic matter in the soil.", "Greater abundance and variety of pollinators and beneficial insects.", "Improved water retention and reduced soil erosion."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"offsetting\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Canceling out", "Delaying", "Increasing", "Documenting"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"disproportionately\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Rarely", "Temporarily", "Unevenly", "Equally"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"This mismatch\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["The gap between organic farming's soil benefits and its biodiversity benefits.", "The difference between consumers' belief that organic food is more nutritious and the modest nutritional differences actually found in studies.", "The difference in certification costs between small and large farms.", "The gap between organic and conventional crop yields."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what can be inferred about the overall environmental impact of organic farming's lower yields?", options: ["Lower yields prove that organic farming is always worse for the environment than conventional farming in every case.", "Lower yields have no relevance to the environmental sustainability of organic farming.", "Because lower yields may require more land to produce the same amount of food, some environmental gains achieved per unit of farmland could be reduced when the full land footprint is considered.", "Lower yields only affect cereal grains and have no broader implications for land use."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the claim that organic farming is more sustainable than conventional farming?", options: ["Dismissive, arguing that organic farming offers no real environmental advantages at all.", "Balanced and analytical, acknowledging genuine benefits while highlighting structural limitations that complicate any simple conclusion.", "Uncertain to the point of offering no clear evidence or discussion on the topic.", "Strongly supportive, presenting organic farming as unambiguously superior in every respect."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Rehabilitating Dietary Fat", paragraphs: ["For much of the latter half of the twentieth century, dietary fat occupied an almost uniformly negative position in nutritional guidance, blamed broadly for rising rates of heart disease and obesity across industrialized nations. Public health recommendations issued during this period urged consumers toward low-fat alternatives across nearly all food categories, a shift that reshaped food manufacturing, grocery shelves, and household eating habits for decades. More recent research, however, has substantially complicated this once-uniform narrative, revealing that the relationship between fat consumption and health outcomes depends considerably on the specific type of fat in question rather than on total fat intake alone.", "Central to this revised understanding is the distinction between saturated, unsaturated, and trans fats, each of which appears to influence cardiovascular health quite differently. Saturated fats, found predominantly in animal products and certain tropical oils, tend to raise levels of low-density lipoprotein cholesterol, a marker consistently associated with elevated cardiovascular risk. Unsaturated fats, by contrast—particularly the polyunsaturated and monounsaturated varieties found in fish, nuts, and olive oil—appear to exert a broadly protective effect, in some studies modestly lowering cardiovascular risk when substituted for saturated fat rather than for carbohydrates. Trans fats, largely artificial and produced through industrial hydrogenation, have emerged as the clearest villain among the three, sufficiently harmful that many countries have since restricted or banned their use in commercial food production.", "Beyond cardiovascular effects, fats serve functions that low-fat dietary guidance of earlier decades tended to overlook. Certain vitamins—A, D, E, and K—are fat-soluble, meaning the body cannot absorb them efficiently without adequate dietary fat present in the same meal. Fat also plays a structural role in cellular membranes throughout the body and serves as a precursor for various hormones, including several involved in reproductive and metabolic regulation. Diets that restrict fat too aggressively, some researchers now argue, may inadvertently compromise these functions even while nominally succeeding at reducing overall caloric intake.", "The replacement problem represents perhaps the most consequential complication overlooked by earlier low-fat guidance. When food manufacturers reduced fat content to meet consumer demand and regulatory encouragement, palatability was frequently preserved by increasing sugar and refined carbohydrate content instead. Several researchers examining population-level dietary trends have argued that this substitution —rather than fat reduction itself—may have contributed meaningfully to rising obesity and metabolic disease rates during the very decades when fat consumption was declining, an outcome nearly opposite to what low-fat guidance had intended to achieve.", "Omega-3 and omega-6 fatty acids, both classified as essential because the human body cannot synthesize them independently, have attracted particular scientific interest in recent years. Omega-3s, concentrated in fatty fish, flaxseed, and walnuts, have been associated in numerous studies with reduced inflammation and modestly improved cardiovascular outcomes. Omega-6s, while also essential, are already abundant in most modern diets—found extensively in vegetable oils and processed foods—leading some researchers to argue that the typical dietary ratio between the two has become skewed relative to ratios believed to have characterized earlier human diets, though the clinical significance of this particular ratio remains genuinely contested among specialists.", "Contemporary dietary guidelines in many countries now reflect this more differentiated understanding, generally encouraging the replacement of saturated and trans fats with unsaturated alternatives, rather than reducing total fat intake indiscriminately. Nevertheless, translating this nuanced, fat-type-specific message into consistent public understanding and everyday food choices has proven considerably more difficult than the earlier, simpler \"fat is bad\" message it replaced, since nutrition labels and marketing claims do not always make the underlying distinctions easy for ordinary consumers to identify at the point of purchase.", "Taken together, the accumulated evidence suggests that dietary fat's role in human health cannot be reduced to a single verdict, whether favorable or unfavorable. Its effects depend substantially on which specific fats are consumed, what they replace in the overall diet, and how they interact with other nutritional and lifestyle factors—an interdependence that continues to complicate efforts to translate nutrition science into simple, universally applicable dietary advice."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage argues that all dietary fat should be avoided to reduce cardiovascular disease risk.", "The passage claims that omega-3 and omega-6 fatty acids have identical effects on human health.", "The passage focuses mainly on describing the chemical structure of saturated and unsaturated fats.", "The passage explains how scientific understanding of dietary fat has shifted from a uniform condemnation toward a more nuanced view that depends on fat type, biological function, and what fat replaces in the diet."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do the three main types of fat differ in their effects on cardiovascular health?", options: ["Unsaturated fats are the only type of fat associated with any cardiovascular risk at all.", "Saturated fats, found predominantly in animal products and certain tropical oils, tend to raise levels of low-density lipoprotein cholesterol.", "Saturated fats tend to raise a cardiovascular risk marker, unsaturated fats appear modestly protective when replacing saturated fat, and trans fats are considered the most consistently harmful of the three.", "All three types of fat have been shown to raise cardiovascular risk to roughly the same degree."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what biological functions of fat did earlier low-fat guidance tend to overlook?", options: ["Fat is the only nutrient required for the human body to produce red blood cells.", "Fat has no biological function beyond providing calories for energy.", "Fat also plays a structural role in cellular membranes throughout the body.", "Fat enables absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, contributes to cell membrane structure, and serves as a precursor for certain hormones."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what unintended consequence resulted from manufacturers reducing fat content in food products?", options: ["Palatability was frequently preserved by increasing sugar and refined carbohydrate content instead.", "Food manufacturers stopped producing low-fat products entirely due to consumer complaints.", "Reducing fat content had no measurable effect on the taste or composition of processed foods.", "Manufacturers often increased sugar and refined carbohydrates to maintain taste, a substitution some researchers link to rising obesity and metabolic disease during that period."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern have some researchers raised about the modern dietary ratio of omega-6 to omega-3 fatty acids?", options: ["Omega-6 fatty acids have been definitively proven to cause inflammation in all individuals who consume them.", "Omega-6s, while also essential, are already abundant in most modern diets—found extensively in vegetable oils and processed foods.", "The ratio between omega-6 and omega-3 intake may have become skewed relative to earlier human diets, although the clinical importance of this remains genuinely debated.", "Omega-3 fatty acids are now considered unnecessary for human health according to recent research."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a function or effect of dietary fat?", options: ["Enabling absorption of fat-soluble vitamins such as A, D, E, and K.", "Playing a structural role in cellular membranes.", "Serving as a precursor for certain hormones.", "The claim that fat consumption has been proven to have no relationship whatsoever with cardiovascular disease."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"palatability\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Shelf life", "Nutritional value", "Taste appeal", "Production cost"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"skewed\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Measured", "Balanced", "Reduced", "Imbalanced"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"this substitution\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The increase in sugar and refined carbohydrates used to replace reduced fat content in food products.", "The replacement of saturated fat with unsaturated fat in dietary guidelines.", "The replacement of trans fats with omega-3 fatty acids in commercial food production.", "The substitution of low-density lipoprotein cholesterol for high-density lipoprotein cholesterol."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view on why low-fat guidance may have failed to improve public health as intended?", options: ["The author claims that scientists have now proven fat consumption has no relationship to any health outcome whatsoever.", "The author argues that low-fat guidance succeeded completely and that any remaining health problems are unrelated to dietary changes.", "The author suggests that the guidance's shortcoming may lie less in targeting fat itself and more in the guidance being entirely correct, with obesity increases caused by unrelated factors never discussed in the passage.", "The author suggests that the guidance's shortcoming may lie less in targeting fat itself and more in failing to anticipate that fat reduction would often be offset by increased sugar and refined carbohydrate consumption."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Genomic Research and the Persistence of Interpretive Uncertainty", paragraphs: ["The sequencing of the human genome, completed in draft form at the turn of the millennium and refined considerably since, was widely anticipated to usher in an era in which genetic information could be translated directly into predictive insight about individual health, ancestry, and even behavior. Two decades of subsequent research have complicated this expectation substantially, revealing that possessing a complete genomic sequence, while an extraordinary technical achievement, does not by itself resolve the far more difficult interpretive question of what most of that sequence actually does.", "A foundational complication concerns the sheer scale of genetic variation between individuals, most of which carries no discernible functional consequence whatsoever. Distinguishing the comparatively rare variants that meaningfully influence disease risk from the vast background of functionally neutral variation requires statistical approaches capable of detecting subtle associations across enormous datasets, a methodological challenge that has driven the development of genome-wide association studies, or GWAS, now standard in the field. Even successful GWAS findings, however, typically identify variants associated with only a modest fraction of a given trait's overall heritability, a phenomenon researchers have termed \"missing heritability\" and one whose underlying explanation remains only partially resolved.", "Polygenic risk scores, which aggregate the small individual effects of many genetic variants into a single composite estimate of disease susceptibility, have emerged as one prominent response to this challenge. [1] Proponents argue that these scores may eventually allow clinicians to identify high-risk individuals well before symptoms emerge, enabling earlier intervention for conditions ranging from cardiovascular disease to certain cancers. [2] Critics counter that the predictive accuracy of current polygenic risk scores varies considerably depending on the ancestral population in which the underlying research was conducted, since the overwhelming majority of large-scale genomic studies to date have drawn primarily on populations of European ancestry. [3] This imbalance does not necessarily invalidate the scores for other populations outright, but it does mean their predictive reliability cannot simply be assumed to transfer uniformly across ancestral groups without population-specific validation. [4]", "Epigenetics, the study of heritable changes in gene expression that occur without alteration to the underlying DNA sequence itself, has introduced a further layer of interpretive complexity. Environmental factors, ranging from nutrition to chronic stress exposure, appear capable of modifying which genes are actively expressed, sometimes in ways that persist across cell divisions and, in certain documented cases, across generations. This finding complicates any straightforwardly deterministic reading of genetic sequence data, suggesting that a given genotype does not correspond to a single, fixed phenotypic outcome but rather to a range of possible outcomes shaped considerably by environmental context.", "The commercialization of direct-to-consumer genetic testing has brought these interpretive uncertainties into direct, and not always well-managed, contact with the general public. Companies offering ancestry and health-risk information based on consumer-submitted DNA samples frequently present probabilistic findings using language that, however unintentionally, conveys greater certainty than the underlying science actually supports, a tendency some researchers argue may lead consumers to overestimate the deterministic power of their genetic results relative to environmental and lifestyle factors that often carry comparable or greater influence.", "Ethical and legal frameworks governing genomic data have struggled to keep pace with the technology's rapid advancement. Questions concerning data ownership, the permissible scope of genetic discrimination by insurers or employers, and the handling of incidental findings—unexpected genetic information discovered during testing for an unrelated purpose—remain unevenly addressed across jurisdictions, with some countries maintaining comparatively robust protections and others offering consumers considerably less legal recourse should their genetic data be misused or inadequately secured.", "None of these complications diminishes genomic research's substantial scientific and clinical value; rare monogenic disorders, in particular, have been diagnosed and in some cases treated with a precision unimaginable before sequencing technology matured. Rather, these complications suggest that the field's early promise of straightforward genetic prediction was, in retrospect, considerably oversimplified, and that meaningful clinical translation will likely continue to depend on integrating genomic data with environmental, epigenetic, and population-specific context rather than treating sequence data as a self-sufficient predictive instrument.", "Whether public understanding and regulatory frameworks will adapt at a pace commensurate with the science itself remains an open question, one this passage does not claim to resolve, though the gap between genomic capability and interpretive maturity appears, on the available evidence, unlikely to close quickly."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The passage concludes that polygenic risk scores are now fully reliable across all human populations.", "The passage explores why genomic research has proven more interpretively complex than early expectations suggested, examining statistical, biological, commercial, and legal dimensions of this uncertainty.", "The passage argues that genomic sequencing has failed to produce any meaningful clinical benefit since its completion.", "The passage focuses primarily on describing the technical process by which the human genome was originally sequenced."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the phenomenon of \"missing heritability\" refer to?", options: ["The loss of genetic material that occurs naturally during human reproduction across generations.", "The complete inability of GWAS to detect any genetic variants associated with disease risk.", "A methodological challenge that has driven the development of genome-wide association studies, or GWAS, now standard in the field.", "The gap between the modest fraction of a trait's heritability explained by identified genetic variants and the trait's total estimated heritability, a gap not yet fully explained."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what limitation affects the reliability of current polygenic risk scores?", options: ["Polygenic risk scores are equally reliable across all ancestral populations because they aggregate many genetic variants.", "Polygenic risk scores have been proven completely inaccurate for every population studied to date.", "Critics counter that the predictive accuracy of current polygenic risk scores varies considerably depending on the ancestral population in which the underlying research was conducted.", "Because most large-scale genomic studies have relied primarily on populations of European ancestry, the predictive reliability of these scores cannot be assumed to transfer uniformly to other ancestral groups without further validation."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does epigenetics suggest about the relationship between genotype and observable traits?", options: ["A given genetic sequence does not guarantee one fixed outcome, since environmental factors can influence gene expression in ways that sometimes persist across generations.", "Environmental factors have been shown to have no measurable influence on gene expression.", "Epigenetic changes always alter the underlying DNA sequence itself, unlike ordinary genetic mutations.", "Environmental factors, ranging from nutrition to chronic stress exposure, appear capable of modifying which genes are actively expressed."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern do researchers raise about direct-to-consumer genetic testing companies?", options: ["Companies offering ancestry and health-risk information based on consumer-submitted DNA samples frequently present probabilistic findings using language that conveys greater certainty than the underlying science supports.", "Direct-to-consumer genetic testing companies have been proven to fabricate all of their reported results.", "Consumers who use these services are legally required to share their results with insurance companies.", "These companies may lead consumers to overestimate how deterministic their genetic results are, relative to environmental and lifestyle factors that often carry comparable influence."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an unresolved legal or ethical issue surrounding genomic data?", options: ["The claim that every country has already established identical, fully robust legal protections for genetic data.", "The handling of incidental findings discovered during unrelated genetic testing.", "The permissible scope of genetic discrimination by insurers or employers.", "Data ownership."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"deterministic\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Reversible over time", "Difficult to measure", "Random and unpredictable", "Fixed and predictable"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"commensurate\" in paragraph 8 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Delayed", "Unrelated", "Proportional", "Opposed"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"This imbalance\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["The disproportionate reliance of large-scale genomic studies on populations of European ancestry.", "The difference between epigenetic and genetic modification of traits.", "The gap between identified genetic variants and a trait's total heritability.", "The imbalance between rare monogenic disorders and common polygenic conditions."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on the passage as a whole, what can be inferred about the author's view on the future clinical value of genomic research?", options: ["The author argues that genomic research has provided no clinical value whatsoever beyond ancestry testing.", "The author claims that all remaining interpretive challenges in genomics have already been resolved by current polygenic risk score methods.", "The author suggests that genomic research retains substantial value, particularly for rare monogenic disorders, but implies that meaningful broader clinical translation will likely require integrating genetic data with environmental and population-specific context, a process unlikely to happen quickly.", "The author suggests that genomic research retains substantial value, particularly for rare monogenic disorders, and predicts confidently that regulatory frameworks will fully resolve all interpretive uncertainties within the next few years."], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
